{
  /*
    The names that go with the seven planetary squares: each square's
    divine names, the planets' intelligences and spirits, Venus'
    intelligences (the Bne Seraphim) and the Moon's intelligence of
    intelligences and spirit of spirits, as each witness prints them
    (plan 093). Where a printed number is not the sum of the printed
    Hebrew, planetaryNameExceptions.json5, beside this file, says so and
    whose the difference is.

    The witnesses, by the keys `witnesses` uses. `leaf` is the
    archive.org BookReader leaf, archive.org/details/<item>/page/n<N>:
      agrippa1533: Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres (Cologne,
        1533), Book II, chapter 22, archive.org DeOccultaPhilosophiaLoc1533:
        the names of Saturn to Mercury on n163, the Moon's on n164.
      agrippa1651: Three Books of Occult Philosophy (London, 1651), p. 243,
        archive.org b30335231, n256. The margin is cut there, and the
        Moon's long names are read in the EEBO copy,
        bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_three-books-of-occult-ph_agrippa-henricus-cornel_1651,
        whose leaves are b30335231's plus 15 (n271). A reading made in
        that copy names it in `item`, and its `leaf` is that copy's.
      barrett1801: Barrett, The Magus (London, 1801), I.ii.28, archive.org
        b22006795 (Wellcome), pp. 146–147, n181–n182.
      crowley777: Crowley, 777 Revised (London, 1955), Table III, p. 20,
        read in Celephaïs Press's electronic edition, archive.org
        aleister-crowley-liber-777, n27. It is in copyright: spellings and
        numbers only, as facts. We have not seen a scan of the first
        edition (1909), nor of 1955's printed page.
      regardie1940: Regardie, The Golden Dawn, vol. 4 (Chicago, 1940),
        pp. 81–86, archive.org IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol4-1940,
        n75–n80: the names in roman capitals only, as the plates' captions,
        with no Hebrew and no numbers. In copyright where the site is read
        (plan 091): the names only, as facts.

    Who read them, and how: Claude (Opus 5.5) read the names by eye on
    the scans on 19 November 2026, a research agent and then a reviewer,
    knowing the values to expect; the sums were computed, not read. It is
    one pass, recorded in plan 093's appendix and moved here unchanged.

    A witness's reading holds what it prints, every field optional: `he`,
    the Hebrew, finals as printed; `roman`, the name in roman type;
    `caption`, the words it prints with the name to say what it is;
    `number`, the number printed beside it; `count`, how that number is
    reached where it is not the plain sum (`finals`: the final letters at
    500–900; `pairs`: each pair of letters makes it, as Agrippa's
    "octonarius extensus"); `note`, an uncertain reading or a detail of
    the page. Where the appendix gives a witness as "the same as 1533",
    the Hebrew, the number and a romanisation that is only the name are
    repeated; a Latin caption, or a romanisation with Latin words in it,
    is not, since an English printing does not print the Latin.

    `name.roman` is the name the site shows. For the fourteen names the
    planets already named it is the id capitalised, which is the Golden
    Dawn's form (Bartzabel, Chasmodai); for the rest, Agrippa's 1533
    romanisation, or Barrett's for Venus' אהא. Three divine names no
    witness romanises, and theirs are our letter-for-letter transcriptions
    of the Hebrew, with no vowels supplied (`note` says so).

    Shelachel, the Moon's `intelligenceId`, has no witness here: it is a
    name found in present-day sources, which planets.json5's header
    explains, and there is nothing to trace.
   */

